# English meanings written for this site (original wording, not copied from any translation).
CH1 = [
 "Sanjaya, on the sacred field of Kurukshetra, my sons and the sons of Pandu gathered, eager to fight. What did they do?",
 "Seeing the Pandava army drawn up for battle, King Duryodhana went to his teacher Drona and spoke.",
 "Look, teacher, at this mighty army of the sons of Pandu, arrayed by your own gifted student, the son of Drupada.",
 "Here are heroes and great archers, equal to Bhima and Arjuna in battle: Yuyudhana, Virata and the great chariot-warrior Drupada;",
 "Dhrishtaketu, Chekitana and the valiant king of Kashi; Purujit, Kuntibhoja and Shaibya, the best of men;",
 "the bold Yudhamanyu, the brave Uttamaujas, the son of Subhadra and the sons of Draupadi, all of them great chariot-warriors.",
 "Now hear, best of the twice-born, about the leading warriors on our side. I name the commanders of my army for you.",
 "Yourself, Bhishma, Karna and Kripa, victorious in battle; Ashvatthama, Vikarna and the son of Somadatta.",
 "And many other heroes ready to give their lives for me, armed with many kinds of weapons, all skilled in war.",
 "Our army, guarded by Bhishma, is beyond measure; theirs, guarded by Bhima, is limited. (Readings of this verse differ: some take it to mean the opposite.)",
 "So all of you, holding your positions at every point of entry, must protect Bhishma above all.",
 "Then Bhishma, the mighty grandsire and eldest of the Kurus, roared like a lion and blew his conch, filling Duryodhana with joy.",
 "At once conches and kettledrums, cymbals, drums and horns blared together, and the sound was tremendous.",
 "Then, standing in their great chariot yoked to white horses, Krishna and Arjuna blew their divine conches.",
 "Krishna blew Panchajanya, Arjuna blew Devadatta, and Bhima, doer of fearsome deeds, blew his great conch Paundra.",
 "King Yudhishthira, son of Kunti, blew Anantavijaya; Nakula and Sahadeva blew Sughosha and Manipushpaka.",
 "The king of Kashi, a supreme archer; Shikhandi, the great chariot-warrior; Dhrishtadyumna, Virata and the unconquered Satyaki;",
 "Drupada, the sons of Draupadi and the mighty-armed son of Subhadra each blew their own conch, O lord of the earth.",
 "That roar echoed through earth and sky and tore at the hearts of Dhritarashtra's sons.",
 "Then, seeing Dhritarashtra's sons arrayed for battle and the weapons about to fly, Arjuna, whose banner bore Hanuman, lifted his bow",
 "and spoke to Krishna. Arjuna said: Achyuta, draw my chariot up between the two armies,",
 "so that I can look at those who stand here eager to fight, and see whom I must face in this battle.",
 "Let me see the men gathered here to fight, wanting to please the ill-minded son of Dhritarashtra.",
 "Asked this by Arjuna, Krishna drove the finest of chariots between the two armies,",
 "in front of Bhishma, Drona and all the kings of the earth, and said: Partha, see all these Kurus gathered here.",
 "There Arjuna saw, standing in both armies, fathers and grandfathers, teachers, uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons and friends,",
 "fathers-in-law and well-wishers. Seeing all these kinsmen arrayed before him, Arjuna, son of Kunti,",
 "was overcome by deep compassion and spoke in sorrow. Arjuna said: Krishna, seeing my own people eager to fight,",
 "my limbs give way, my mouth goes dry, my body trembles and my hair stands on end.",
 "The Gandiva slips from my hand and my skin is burning. I cannot stand steady; my mind is reeling.",
 "I see evil omens, Keshava, and I see no good in killing my own kin in battle.",
 "I do not want victory, Krishna, nor a kingdom, nor pleasures. What use is a kingdom to us, Govinda, or pleasure, or even life itself?",
 "Those for whose sake we want a kingdom, enjoyment and pleasure stand here in battle, ready to give up their lives and their wealth:",
 "teachers, fathers, sons and grandfathers, uncles, fathers-in-law, grandsons, brothers-in-law and other kinsmen.",
 "I do not want to kill them, Madhusudana, even if they kill me; not for rule over the three worlds, let alone for this earth.",
 "What joy could we have from killing the sons of Dhritarashtra, Janardana? Only sin would come to us from slaying these aggressors.",
 "So it is not right for us to kill the sons of Dhritarashtra, our own kinsmen. How could we be happy, Madhava, after killing our own people?",
 "Even if they, their minds overcome by greed, see no wrong in destroying a family or in betraying friends,",
 "why should we, who see clearly the evil of destroying a family, not learn to turn away from this sin, Janardana?",
 "When a family is destroyed, its timeless traditions perish; and when tradition perishes, disorder overtakes the whole family.",
 "When disorder prevails, Krishna, the women of the family are led astray, and from that comes the mixing of social orders.",
 "That confusion drags the family and its destroyers down to hell, and their ancestors fall too, deprived of the offerings of food and water.",
 "Through these wrongs of those who destroy families and cause the mixing of orders, the age-old duties of community and family are lost.",
 "We have heard, Janardana, that people whose family traditions are destroyed live in hell for a long time.",
 "Alas, how strange that we are set on committing a great sin, ready to kill our own kin out of greed for the pleasures of a kingdom.",
 "It would be better for me if the sons of Dhritarashtra, weapons in hand, killed me in battle, unarmed and not fighting back.",
 "Having spoken this on the battlefield, Arjuna put aside his bow and arrows and sank down on the seat of the chariot, his mind overwhelmed by grief.",
]
CH2 = [
 "To Arjuna, overwhelmed by pity, his eyes full of tears and his heart sinking, Madhusudana spoke these words.",
 "Arjuna, where has this weakness come from at such a moment? It does not befit a noble person, and it leads neither to heaven nor to honour.",
 "Do not give in to this unmanliness, Partha; it does not suit you. Throw off this petty weakness of heart and stand up, scorcher of foes.",
 "Madhusudana, how can I shoot arrows in battle at Bhishma and Drona? They deserve my reverence, slayer of foes.",
 "It would be better to live in this world on alms than to kill these noble teachers. If I killed them, every pleasure and every gain I enjoyed here would be stained with blood.",
 "We do not even know which is better for us, that we conquer them or that they conquer us. The sons of Dhritarashtra stand before us; if we kill them, we would not want to live.",
 "My nature is overcome by weakness and pity, and my mind is confused about my duty. I ask you: tell me for certain what is best. I am your disciple and I have come to you for refuge. Teach me.",
 "I see nothing that could drive away this grief that is drying up my senses, not even an unrivalled, prosperous kingdom on earth or lordship over the gods.",
 "Having said this to Krishna, Arjuna, the scorcher of foes, told Govinda, \"I will not fight,\" and fell silent.",
 "Then, between the two armies, Krishna, as if smiling, spoke these words to the grieving Arjuna.",
 "You grieve for those who need no grief, and yet you speak words that sound wise. The wise grieve neither for the living nor for the dead.",
 "There was never a time when I did not exist, nor you, nor these kings; and there will never be a time when any of us ceases to be.",
 "Just as the soul passes through childhood, youth and old age in this body, so it passes into another body. The steady are not confused by this.",
 "Contact of the senses with their objects brings cold and heat, pleasure and pain. They come and go and do not last. Bear them patiently, Arjuna.",
 "The person these do not disturb, who stays the same in pleasure and pain, steady and wise, is fit for immortality.",
 "The unreal never comes to be, and the real never stops being. Those who see the truth have understood both.",
 "Know that what pervades this whole body is indestructible. No one can destroy what is imperishable.",
 "These bodies of the eternal, indestructible and immeasurable soul are said to come to an end. Therefore fight, Arjuna.",
 "One who thinks the soul is a killer, and one who thinks it is killed, both fail to understand. It neither kills nor is killed.",
 "The soul is never born and never dies. It did not come into being and will not stop being. Unborn, eternal, everlasting and ancient, it is not killed when the body is killed.",
 "Partha, how could one who knows the soul to be indestructible, eternal, unborn and unchanging kill anyone, or make anyone kill?",
 "Just as a person throws away worn-out clothes and puts on new ones, the soul throws away worn-out bodies and enters new ones.",
 "Weapons cannot cut it, fire cannot burn it, water cannot wet it and wind cannot dry it.",
 "It cannot be cut, burned, wetted or dried. It is eternal, present everywhere, stable, unmoving and timeless.",
 "It is called unmanifest, beyond thought and unchanging. Knowing it to be so, you should not grieve.",
 "And even if you think the soul is constantly born and constantly dies, mighty-armed one, you still have no reason to grieve.",
 "Death is certain for whoever is born, and birth is certain for whoever dies. So you should not grieve over what cannot be avoided.",
 "Beings are unmanifest before birth, manifest in the middle, and unmanifest again at death. What is there to mourn, Arjuna?",
 "Some see the soul as a wonder, some speak of it as a wonder, and some hear of it as a wonder; yet even after hearing, no one truly knows it.",
 "The soul that lives in every body can never be killed, Arjuna. So you should not grieve for any being.",
 "Considering your own duty as well, you should not waver. For a warrior there is nothing better than a war fought for what is right.",
 "Fortunate are the warriors, Partha, to whom such a war comes unsought, like an open door to heaven.",
 "But if you refuse to fight this righteous war, you will give up your duty and your honour, and you will incur sin.",
 "People will tell of your disgrace for ever, and for someone who has been honoured, disgrace is worse than death.",
 "The great warriors will think you fled the battle out of fear, and those who held you in high regard will think little of you.",
 "Your enemies will say many unspeakable things, mocking your strength. What could be more painful than that?",
 "If you are killed, you will reach heaven; if you win, you will enjoy the earth. So stand up, son of Kunti, determined to fight.",
 "Treating pleasure and pain, gain and loss, victory and defeat alike, get ready for battle. That way you will incur no sin.",
 "I have explained this wisdom to you in terms of Sankhya; now hear it in terms of yoga. United with this understanding, Partha, you will break the bonds of karma.",
 "On this path no effort is ever wasted and no harm is done. Even a little of this practice protects you from great fear.",
 "Here the determined mind is focused on one thing, Arjuna; the thoughts of the undetermined are endless and branch in every direction.",
 "Those without discernment speak in flowery words, delighting in the letter of the Vedas and saying there is nothing more, Partha.",
 "Full of desires and intent on heaven, they recommend many elaborate rites aimed at pleasure and power, which only bring rebirth as the fruit of action.",
 "For those attached to pleasure and power, whose minds are carried away by such talk, the determined, focused mind does not arise in meditation.",
 "The Vedas deal with the three qualities of nature. Rise above them, Arjuna: free from the pairs of opposites, always grounded in truth, unconcerned with getting and keeping, and centred in the Self.",
 "To someone who knows Brahman, all the Vedas are as useful as a small pond in a land flooded with water on every side.",
 "You have a right to your work, but never to its fruits. Do not let the fruits of action be your motive, and do not cling to inaction either.",
 "Do your work grounded in yoga, Dhananjaya, letting go of attachment and staying even-minded in success and failure. This evenness of mind is called yoga.",
 "Work done for its rewards is far below work guided by wisdom, Dhananjaya. Take refuge in that wisdom; those who work only for the fruits are to be pitied.",
 "One united with this wisdom leaves behind both good and bad deeds even in this life. So commit yourself to yoga: yoga is skill in action.",
 "The wise, united with this understanding, let go of the fruits of action, are freed from the bonds of birth, and reach the state beyond all sorrow.",
 "When your understanding crosses the swamp of delusion, you will no longer care about what you have heard or what is still to be heard.",
 "When your understanding, unsettled by conflicting teachings, stands firm and unmoving in meditation, you will reach yoga.",
 "Keshava, how would you describe someone whose wisdom is steady and who is absorbed in meditation? How does such a person speak, sit and walk?",
 "When a person lets go of every desire of the mind, Partha, and is content in the Self through the Self, they are said to have steady wisdom.",
 "One who is not shaken by sorrow, does not long for pleasure, and is free from attachment, fear and anger is called a sage of steady mind.",
 "One who is unattached everywhere, who neither celebrates good fortune nor resents bad, has wisdom that is firmly established.",
 "When, like a tortoise drawing in its limbs, a person withdraws the senses from their objects, their wisdom is firmly established.",
 "Sense objects fall away from one who abstains from them, but the taste for them remains; even that taste disappears once the Supreme is seen.",
 "The restless senses, son of Kunti, can forcibly carry away the mind even of a wise person who is striving for control.",
 "Restraining them all, one should sit in yoga, devoted to Me. Whoever has their senses under control has steady wisdom.",
 "When a person dwells on sense objects, attachment to them grows; from attachment comes desire, and from desire comes anger.",
 "From anger comes delusion; from delusion, a confused memory; from a confused memory, the loss of reason; and when reason is lost, the person is ruined.",
 "But a disciplined person who moves among sense objects with senses under control, free from attraction and aversion, reaches inner calm.",
 "In that calm all sorrows come to an end, and the understanding of the calm-hearted quickly becomes steady.",
 "One who is not disciplined has no wisdom and no focus; without focus there is no peace, and without peace, how can there be happiness?",
 "Just as a strong wind sweeps a boat off course on the water, a mind that follows the wandering senses carries away one's wisdom.",
 "So, mighty-armed one, the person whose senses are fully withdrawn from their objects has wisdom that is firmly established.",
 "What is night for all beings is wakefulness for the self-disciplined; and what is wakefulness for all beings is night for the sage who truly sees.",
 "Just as the ocean stays full and still while rivers keep flowing into it, the person into whom desires flow in the same way finds peace, not the one who chases desires.",
 "Whoever lets go of all desires and lives free from longing, without the sense of \"mine\" and without ego, finds peace.",
 "This is the state of Brahman, Partha. Having reached it, one is no longer deluded; established in it even at the time of death, one attains the peace of Brahman.",
]

CH3 = [
 "Janardana, if you hold that understanding is greater than action, then why, Keshava, do you urge me into this terrible deed?",
 "With words that seem to point two ways, you confuse my mind. Tell me, decisively, the one path by which I may reach the highest good.",
 "Sinless one, as I said before, there are two paths in this world: the path of knowledge for those given to contemplation, and the path of action for those given to yoga.",
 "No one reaches freedom from action simply by not beginning it, and no one attains perfection by renunciation alone.",
 "No one can remain without acting even for a moment. Everyone is driven to act, helplessly, by the qualities born of nature.",
 "Whoever restrains the organs of action but sits dwelling on sense objects in the mind is deluded, and is called a hypocrite.",
 "But whoever governs the senses with the mind, Arjuna, and without attachment takes up the yoga of action with the organs of action, excels.",
 "Do the work that is yours, for action is better than inaction. Without action, even the upkeep of your body would not be possible.",
 "This world is bound by action, except action done as an offering (yajna). So, son of Kunti, act for that purpose, free from attachment.",
 "In the beginning the Creator brought forth beings together with sacrifice and said: by this you shall flourish; let it be the cow that grants your wishes.",
 "Nourish the gods with this, and let the gods nourish you. Nourishing one another, you will reach the highest good.",
 "Nourished by sacrifice, the gods will give you the enjoyments you desire. Whoever enjoys their gifts without offering anything back is simply a thief.",
 "The good, who eat what remains after the offering, are freed of all wrongs; but those who cook only for themselves eat sin.",
 "Beings come from food, food comes from rain, rain comes from sacrifice, and sacrifice is born of action.",
 "Know that action arises from the Veda, and the Veda from the Imperishable. So the all-pervading Brahman is ever present in sacrifice.",
 "Whoever does not keep turning the wheel set in motion this way lives in sin, delighting only in the senses; that life is lived in vain, Partha.",
 "But for one who delights in the Self alone, who is satisfied in the Self and content in the Self, there is nothing left that must be done.",
 "Such a person has nothing to gain by acting here, nothing to lose by not acting, and depends on no being for anything.",
 "So always do the work that must be done, without attachment. By acting without attachment, a person reaches the Supreme.",
 "Janaka and others reached perfection through action alone. Keeping in view the welfare of the world (lokasangraha), you too should act.",
 "Whatever the best person does, others follow. Whatever standard they set, the world goes by it.",
 "Partha, there is nothing in the three worlds that I must do, nothing I lack that I need to gain; and yet I keep on acting.",
 "For if I ever stopped acting, tirelessly, people everywhere would follow my path, Partha.",
 "If I did not act, these worlds would fall into ruin; I would be the cause of chaos and would destroy these beings.",
 "As the unwise act attached to their work, Bharata, so the wise should act, unattached, wishing to hold the world together.",
 "The wise should not unsettle the minds of the ignorant who are attached to action. Acting with discipline themselves, they should inspire others to do all their work gladly.",
 "All actions are carried out by the qualities of nature, but one whose mind is clouded by ego thinks, \"I am the doer.\"",
 "But one who knows the truth about the qualities and their workings, mighty-armed, sees that it is qualities moving among qualities, and is not attached.",
 "Those deluded by the qualities of nature cling to their workings. One who knows the whole should not unsettle the slow of mind who know only a part.",
 "Surrendering all actions to me, your mind on the Self, free from hope and from \"mine,\" fight, with the fever gone.",
 "Those who always follow this teaching of mine, with faith and without finding fault, are freed from the bonds of action.",
 "But those who find fault with it and do not follow my teaching, know them to be confused about all knowledge, lost and without discernment.",
 "Even the wise act according to their own nature. All beings follow their nature; what can mere suppression achieve?",
 "Attraction and aversion sit waiting in every sense toward its object. Do not fall under their control; they are robbers on the road.",
 "Better your own dharma, even done imperfectly, than another's done well. Better to die in your own dharma; another's dharma is full of fear.",
 "Then what drives a person to do wrong, Varshneya, even against their own will, as if forced?",
 "It is desire, it is anger, born of the quality of passion (rajas): all-devouring and the great wrongdoer. Know it as the enemy here.",
 "As fire is covered by smoke, a mirror by dust, and an unborn child by the womb, so is this knowledge covered by desire.",
 "Knowledge is veiled, son of Kunti, by this constant enemy of the wise, in the form of desire, a fire that can never be filled.",
 "The senses, the mind and the intellect are said to be its seat. Through them it veils knowledge and deludes the embodied one.",
 "So first master the senses, best of the Bharatas, and slay this sinful thing that destroys knowledge and wisdom.",
 "The senses are high, they say; higher than the senses is the mind; higher than the mind is the intellect; and higher than the intellect is the Self.",
 "Knowing what is higher than the intellect, steady the self by the Self, mighty-armed, and slay this enemy in the form of desire, so hard to conquer.",
]

CH4 = [
 "I taught this imperishable yoga to Vivasvan, the sun; Vivasvan taught it to Manu, and Manu passed it on to Ikshvaku.",
 "Handed down in this way, one to the next, the royal sages knew it. But over a long stretch of time, scorcher of foes, this yoga was lost to the world.",
 "That same ancient yoga I have taught you today, because you are my devotee and my friend. This is the supreme secret.",
 "Your birth came later and Vivasvan's long before. How am I to understand that you taught it in the beginning?",
 "Many births of mine have passed, and of yours too, Arjuna. I know them all; you do not, scorcher of foes.",
 "Though I am unborn and my Self does not perish, though I am lord of all beings, I take charge of my own nature and come into being through my own power.",
 "Whenever dharma declines and adharma rises, Bharata, I bring myself forth.",
 "To protect the good, to destroy those who do evil, and to set dharma firmly in place, I come into being age after age.",
 "Whoever truly knows my divine birth and work, Arjuna, is not born again on leaving the body, but comes to me.",
 "Free from attachment, fear and anger, absorbed in me and taking refuge in me, many, purified by the discipline of knowledge, have reached my state.",
 "However people come to me, so I receive them. All people follow my path, Partha, in every way.",
 "Wanting success in their work, people here worship the gods, for in the human world success born of action comes quickly.",
 "I created the four orders according to the division of qualities and work. Though I am their maker, know me as the non-doer, unchanging.",
 "Actions do not stain me, and I do not long for their fruit. Whoever knows me in this way is not bound by actions.",
 "Knowing this, the seekers of freedom of old also acted. So you too should act, as the ancients did long ago.",
 "What is action? What is inaction? Even the wise are confused here. I will explain action to you; knowing it, you will be freed from harm.",
 "One must understand action, understand wrong action, and understand inaction. Deep and hard to grasp is the way of action.",
 "Whoever sees inaction in action and action in inaction is wise among people; that one is a yogi, doing all that is to be done.",
 "One whose every undertaking is free from desire and selfish intent, whose actions are burned in the fire of knowledge, the wise call learned.",
 "Having given up attachment to the fruit of action, always content and dependent on nothing, such a person, though fully engaged in action, does nothing at all.",
 "Expecting nothing, with mind and body under control, letting go of all possessiveness and doing only the work of the body, one incurs no wrong.",
 "Content with whatever comes unasked, beyond the pairs of opposites, free from envy, even in success and failure, such a person acts and is not bound.",
 "For one free from attachment, liberated, whose mind is anchored in knowledge, who works as an offering, all action melts away entirely.",
 "The offering is Brahman, the oblation is Brahman, poured by Brahman into the fire that is Brahman. Brahman alone is reached by one who sees Brahman in every action.",
 "Some yogis offer sacrifice to the gods alone; others offer the sacrifice itself as an oblation into the fire of Brahman.",
 "Some offer hearing and the other senses into the fires of restraint; others offer sound and the other objects of sense into the fires of the senses.",
 "Others offer all the workings of the senses and of the breath into the fire of self-control, kindled by knowledge.",
 "Some offer wealth, some austerity, some yoga; and others, ascetics of firm vows, offer study of scripture and knowledge.",
 "Others, devoted to breath control, offer the in-breath into the out-breath and the out-breath into the in-breath, restraining the flow of both.",
 "Still others, regulating their food, offer the life-breaths into the life-breaths. All of them know sacrifice, and through sacrifice their impurities are destroyed.",
 "Those who eat the nectar left from sacrifice reach the eternal Brahman. This world is not for one who makes no sacrifice; how then the next, best of the Kurus?",
 "So many kinds of sacrifice are spread out before Brahman. Know them all as born of action; knowing this, you will be set free.",
 "Better than any sacrifice of material things is the sacrifice of knowledge, scorcher of foes. All action, Partha, finds its completion in knowledge.",
 "Learn it by bowing down, by asking questions and by serving. The wise who have seen the truth will teach you this knowledge.",
 "Knowing it, you will never again fall into delusion like this, Pandava. Through it you will see all beings in your Self, and then in me.",
 "Even if you were the worst of all wrongdoers, you would cross over every wrong by the boat of knowledge alone.",
 "As a blazing fire turns firewood to ashes, Arjuna, so the fire of knowledge turns all actions to ashes.",
 "Nothing in this world purifies like knowledge. One perfected in yoga finds it, in time, within oneself.",
 "One with faith, devoted to it, with the senses mastered, gains knowledge; and having gained it, quickly reaches supreme peace.",
 "But the ignorant, the faithless and the doubting perish. For one who doubts, there is neither this world nor the next, nor any happiness.",
 "Actions do not bind one who has renounced action through yoga, whose doubts are cut away by knowledge and who is established in the Self, Dhananjaya.",
 "So, with the sword of knowledge, cut away this doubt born of ignorance that sits in your heart. Take refuge in yoga. Stand up, Bharata!",
]

CH5 = [
 "Krishna, you praise the renunciation of action, and then again its disciplined practice. Tell me decisively which one of the two is better.",
 "Renunciation and the yoga of action both lead to the highest good, but of the two, the yoga of action is better than renouncing action.",
 "Know as a true renunciate the one who neither hates nor craves. Free from the pairs of opposites, mighty-armed, such a person is easily set free from bondage.",
 "Children, not the wise, speak of Sankhya and Yoga as different. Whoever is truly established in even one of them gains the fruit of both.",
 "The state reached by the followers of Sankhya is reached by the yogis too. Whoever sees Sankhya and Yoga as one truly sees.",
 "But renunciation, mighty-armed, is hard to attain without yoga. The sage disciplined in yoga reaches Brahman before long.",
 "Disciplined in yoga, pure in heart, master of self and senses, whose self has become the self of all beings: such a person is not stained even while acting.",
 "\"I do nothing at all,\" thinks the disciplined one who knows the truth, while seeing, hearing, touching, smelling, eating, walking, sleeping, breathing,",
 "speaking, letting go, grasping, opening and closing the eyes, holding firmly that it is only the senses moving among their objects.",
 "Whoever acts offering every action to Brahman, letting go of attachment, is not stained by sin, just as a lotus leaf is not wetted by water.",
 "Yogis act with body, mind and intellect, even with the senses alone, letting go of attachment, to purify the self.",
 "The disciplined one, giving up the fruit of action, reaches lasting peace; the undisciplined, driven by desire and attached to the fruit, is bound.",
 "Renouncing all actions with the mind, the self-controlled embodied one rests happily in the city of nine gates, neither acting nor causing action.",
 "The Lord does not create agency or actions for the world, nor the link between action and its fruit; it is one's own nature that carries these on.",
 "The all-pervading one takes on no one's sin or merit. Knowledge is covered by ignorance, and so beings are deluded.",
 "But for those whose ignorance is destroyed by knowledge of the Self, that knowledge, like the sun, lights up the Supreme.",
 "Minds fixed on That, their self one with That, established in That and devoted to That, their impurities shaken off by knowledge, they go where there is no return.",
 "The wise see with an equal eye a learned and humble brahmin, a cow, an elephant, a dog and one who eats dogs.",
 "Even here, birth and death are conquered by those whose minds rest in equality. Brahman is flawless and the same in all, so they rest in Brahman.",
 "Not elated at getting what is pleasant, not shaken at getting what is unpleasant, steady in understanding and undeluded, the knower of Brahman rests in Brahman.",
 "Unattached to outer contacts, one finds the happiness that is in the Self; joined to Brahman through yoga, one enjoys a happiness that never ends.",
 "Pleasures born of contact are wombs of sorrow; they have a beginning and an end, son of Kunti. The wise do not delight in them.",
 "Whoever, here, before leaving the body, can withstand the surge of desire and anger is disciplined; that person is happy.",
 "Whoever is happy within, delights within and is lit from within, that yogi, one with Brahman, reaches the peace of Brahman (brahma-nirvana).",
 "Sages whose impurities are worn away, whose doubts are cut, who are self-controlled and delight in the good of all beings, attain the peace of Brahman.",
 "For ascetics free from desire and anger, minds controlled, who know the Self, the peace of Brahman is near on every side.",
 "Shutting out outer contacts, fixing the gaze between the brows, making the in-breath and out-breath even as they move within the nostrils,",
 "senses, mind and intellect controlled, intent on freedom, with desire, fear and anger gone: that sage is free forever.",
 "Knowing me as the enjoyer of sacrifices and austerities, the great lord of all the worlds and the friend of all beings, one attains peace.",
]

CH6 = [
 "Whoever does the work that is to be done without depending on its fruit is a renunciate and a yogi; not the one who merely gives up the sacred fire, or all activity.",
 "What they call renunciation, know that to be yoga, Pandava; for no one becomes a yogi without renouncing selfish intention.",
 "For the sage who wishes to climb to yoga, action is said to be the means; for the one who has climbed it, calm is said to be the means.",
 "When one is attached neither to sense objects nor to actions, and has renounced all selfish intention, one is said to have climbed to yoga.",
 "Lift yourself by your own self; do not let yourself sink. For the self alone is the friend of the self, and the self alone is its enemy.",
 "The self is a friend to one who has conquered oneself by oneself; but for one who has not, the self turns hostile, like an enemy.",
 "For one who has conquered the self and is at peace, the supreme Self stays steady, in cold and heat, pleasure and pain, honour and dishonour.",
 "Satisfied with knowledge and its realisation, unshakable, senses conquered, the yogi to whom a clod, a stone and gold are the same is called disciplined.",
 "Excellent is the one who is even-minded toward well-wishers, friends, enemies, the indifferent, the neutral, the hateful and relatives, toward the good and the wicked alike.",
 "The yogi should keep concentrating the mind, staying in seclusion, alone, with mind and body controlled, free from hope and from possessiveness.",
 "In a clean place, setting up a firm seat for oneself, neither too high nor too low, covered with kusha grass, a deerskin and a cloth,",
 "sitting there, making the mind one-pointed, controlling the workings of thought and sense, one should practise yoga to purify the self.",
 "Holding body, head and neck erect and still, steady, gazing at the tip of one's nose and not looking around,",
 "serene, fearless, firm in the vow of celibacy, the mind restrained and thinking of me, one should sit disciplined, intent on me.",
 "Always practising in this way, mind controlled, the yogi reaches the peace that rests in me, the supreme nirvana.",
 "Yoga is not for one who eats too much, nor for one who does not eat at all; not for one who sleeps too much, nor for one who stays awake too long, Arjuna.",
 "For one moderate in food and recreation, measured in action, moderate in sleep and waking, yoga becomes the destroyer of sorrow.",
 "When the disciplined mind rests in the Self alone, free from longing for any desire, one is called united in yoga.",
 "\"As a lamp in a windless place does not flicker\": that is the image for the yogi of controlled mind, practising union with the Self.",
 "Where the mind, restrained by the practice of yoga, comes to rest; where, seeing the Self by the self, one is content in the Self;",
 "where one knows that boundless happiness, grasped by the intellect and beyond the senses, and, established there, never moves from the truth;",
 "having gained which, one thinks no other gain greater; established in which, one is not shaken even by heavy sorrow;",
 "know that to be yoga: the breaking of the bond with sorrow. This yoga should be practised with determination and an undiscouraged mind.",
 "Giving up completely all desires born of selfish intention, restraining the whole crowd of senses on every side with the mind,",
 "little by little one should come to rest, the intellect held firm; making the mind abide in the Self, one should think of nothing else.",
 "Wherever the restless, unsteady mind wanders off, from there one should draw it back and bring it under the control of the Self alone.",
 "Supreme happiness comes to the yogi whose mind is at peace, whose passion is calmed, who is free of impurity and has become one with Brahman.",
 "Always practising in this way, free from impurity, the yogi easily enjoys the boundless happiness of contact with Brahman.",
 "With the self disciplined by yoga, seeing the same everywhere, one sees the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self.",
 "Whoever sees me everywhere and sees everything in me, I am never lost to them, and they are never lost to me.",
 "Whoever, established in oneness, worships me as dwelling in all beings, that yogi lives in me, however they live.",
 "Whoever, by comparison with oneself, sees the same in everything, Arjuna, whether pleasure or pain, is considered the highest yogi.",
 "This yoga of evenness that you have taught, Madhusudana: I cannot see how it can last, because the mind is so restless.",
 "The mind is restless, Krishna, turbulent, strong and stubborn. Controlling it seems to me as hard as controlling the wind.",
 "No doubt, mighty-armed, the mind is restless and hard to restrain. But by practice (abhyasa) and detachment (vairagya), son of Kunti, it can be held.",
 "Yoga is hard to reach for one who is not self-controlled, I agree. But one who is self-controlled and keeps striving can reach it by the right means.",
 "What becomes of one who has faith but does not strive enough, whose mind wanders from yoga, and who fails to reach its perfection, Krishna?",
 "Fallen from both, does such a one not perish like a torn cloud, mighty-armed, without support, lost on the path to Brahman?",
 "Please cut away this doubt of mine completely, Krishna; no one but you can dispel it.",
 "Partha, there is no destruction for such a one, neither here nor hereafter. No one who does good, my friend, ever comes to a bad end.",
 "Having reached the worlds of the righteous and lived there for countless years, the one who fell from yoga is born in the home of the pure and prosperous.",
 "Or they are born into a family of wise yogis, though a birth like that is harder to find in this world.",
 "There they regain the understanding of their former life, and from there they strive again for perfection, joy of the Kurus.",
 "By that earlier practice they are carried along, even without wanting to. Even one who only wishes to know yoga goes beyond the ritual of the Vedas.",
 "But the yogi who strives with effort, cleansed of impurities and perfected over many births, then reaches the highest goal.",
 "The yogi is greater than the ascetics, greater even than the learned, greater than those who perform ritual works. So be a yogi, Arjuna.",
 "And of all yogis, the one who worships me with faith, their inner self absorbed in me, I consider the most united with me.",
]
